eyes come in many shapes and forms  from an owl's eyes which contain  hundreds of millions of light in color  sensing cells  to the eyes of a zooplankton made up of  just a few thousand  although more light-sensitive cells mean  clearer vision  it takes a lot of energy to process this  constant flood of information to the  brain  not every animal has the resources to  spare on 20 20 eyesight  some insects like blowflies for example  have more concentrated areas of  sensitive cells  in the front of them than peripherally  in order to get a clear view of their  surroundings they move just their heads  which uses less energy than moving their  entire body  this led researchers to wonder could  incorporating movement into a vision  system on robots  also save energy they started with a  small but  powerful battery and bluetooth chip next  they had to build their camera  the challenging part was finding the  right lens  they ended up settling on using  different lenses depending on the  situation  one lens was better at capturing images  closer to the camera  while another could focus on distant  objects like buildings  both of these lenses fed into the same  lightweight image sensor  which converted the light entering from  the lens to the image displayed on the  smartphone  the combination of all these parts  weighed only 248 milligrams  less than a pinch of salt although the  resulting video isn't hd  it is clear enough to distinctly see  shapes and forms  in close to real time but to make their  vision truly expansive  scientists rigged up a one-of-a-kind arm  that could move the camera similar to  the way  insects move their heads they could  control the arm from their phone to move  the camera up to 60 degrees  adding to the 30 to 40 degrees field of  vision from just the lens  by stitching together images from the  camera at different positions  researchers could construct panoramic  views of an area  to save even more energy researchers  built an accelerometer onto the vision  system  a device that can detect motion when  turned on the camera only captured video  when the beetle was on the move  for less active beetles this meant the  camera could operate for up to six  hours when the system was mounted onto  an insect sized robot  it saved the bots significant amounts of  energy  just like their live counterparts moving  only the camera took  up to 84 times less energy than moving  the entire robot  to date this is one of the smallest  vision systems that can power itself  this camera will allow researchers to  peer into the habits of insects outside  the lab  especially because it can perform well  in darker environments where many  insects dwell  and for future endeavors researchers  plan to strap the camera onto flying  insects like moths  and pare down the device so it can be  mounted onto even smaller bugs  like flies and bumblebees stay tuned for  a live  action bug's life in the coming years
 

